# /var/lib/propolis-watch/.ssh/authorized_keys for the read-only live watcher.
# Full procedure: docs/operations/live-watch.md.
#
# - This file belongs to the host's real administrative sshd. Connect with `ssh -p <admin-port>`,
#   never to port 22 unless that is the real sshd: on a honeypot host 22 is usually sensor-ssh,
#   the fake SSH sensor, which reads no authorized_keys. Find the real port with
#   `sudo ss -ltnp | grep sshd`.
# - Generate a DEDICATED key on the machine that will read the stream, used for nothing else:
#     ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/propolis_watch -C watch@workstation
#   Paste only its PUBLIC half (~/.ssh/propolis_watch.pub) into the line below, on the honeypot.
#   The private key never leaves the reading machine.
# - The file is root:root 0644, inside the root:root 0755 .ssh directory deploy/provision.sh
#   creates, so the propolis-watch account can read its keys but never add one. sshd accepts that
#   (StrictModes wants the file and the directories above it owned by root or the user and
#   writable by no one else).
# - `restrict` turns off port, agent and X11 forwarding, pty allocation and ~/.ssh/rc for this key.
# - `command=` is a forced command: whatever the client asks to run, sshd runs this instead and puts
#   the client's request in SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND, which propolis-watch reads as its arguments
#   through the same allowlist as its argv (unknown words exit 2). So this key can run the watcher
#   and nothing else.
# - The watcher finds the sensor-log list in /etc/propolis/watch.env, which deploy/watch-env.sh
#   derives from the daemon's propolis.env on every provision and upgrade. Nothing about the list
#   belongs in this file.
restrict,command="/usr/local/bin/propolis-watch" ssh-ed25519 AAAA... watch@workstation
